        "reading": "Separate relevance from weight: identify the proposition that matters (hazard existed at time of injury) and ask whether the footage makes that proposition more or less probable. Under FRE 401 evidence is relevant if it changes the probability of a consequential fact. FRE 402 generally admits relevant evidence unless another authority excludes it. FRE 403 permits exclusion where probative value is substantially outweighed by enumerated risks including unfair prejudice, confusion, misleading the jury, undue delay, wasted time or needless cumulative proof. A weak inference is not automatically unfair prejudice. Authenticate the video independently under FRE 901 and establish temporal conditions before assuming the scene was unchanged.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A retailer offers security-camera footage of a customer slipping. The footage depicts the area thirty minutes after the fall; an employee says cleaning conditions had changed. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Plaintiff argues the footage shows the hazard location and lighting; defendant argues changed conditions undermine probative value and risk misleading jurors. Both should propose a limiting explanation or witness foundation rather than asserting automatic exclusion. Identify the exact proposition supported by Federal Rules of Evidence 401, 402 and 403, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
