        "reading": "Begin with enabling legislation: did Congress authorize the agency to impose the fee? Under APA § 553, legislative rules usually require notice-and-comment unless an exception applies; interpretive rules and general statements of policy have different treatment. Labels chosen by the agency are not conclusive. Under APA § 706 courts decide relevant questions of law. Loper Bright overruled Chevron deference but recognized that statutory delegations can authorize agency discretion within legal boundaries. Distinguish reviewability, standing, final agency action, exhaustion where applicable, and remedy from the merits. Check later controlling authority.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A federal agency emails licensed operators that a new mandatory inspection fee applies immediately. It issued no notice of proposed rulemaking and asserts that the fee merely interprets an older statute. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Agency argues the email clarifies an existing duty and qualifies for an exception; operators argue it imposes a newly binding obligation without required procedure or statutory authorization. Identify the exact proposition supported by 5 U.S.C. §§ 553 and 706; Loper Bright Enterprises v. Raimondo, 603 U.S. 369 (2024), check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
