        "reading": "Analyze duties of loyalty, communication and confidentiality before proposing any disclosure or joint strategy. Under ABA Model Rule 1.7 a concurrent conflict may exist when representation is directly adverse or materially limited; some conflicts are consentable only if the lawyer reasonably believes competent and diligent representation is possible, the representation is not prohibited, and other requirements are satisfied. Informed consent must meet the applicable jurisdiction's requirements, often confirmation in writing. Under Rule 1.6 confidentiality is broad and exceptions are limited. Withdrawal under Rule 1.16 and client-file handling require careful jurisdiction-specific analysis. Do not assume a joint representation permits unrestricted sharing of every private communication.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A lawyer represents two business founders in a dissolution negotiation. Each wants a different allocation of intellectual property, and one privately reveals facts harmful to the other. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: One founder demands disclosure; the other insists it remain private. Consider what engagement terms warned about shared communications and whether the conflict is now nonconsentable. Identify the exact proposition supported by ABA Model Rules of Professional Conduct 1.6, 1.7 and 1.16 (models; jurisdictional rules control), check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
