        "reading": "Start by identifying the taxpayer, property, taxable year and character of the asset. Under IRC § 1001, gain or loss generally compares amount realized with adjusted basis. Amount realized includes money received plus fair market value of property received, subject to applicable rules. Basis may start with cost under § 1012, then be adjusted as required. A note raises valuation and potential installment-method issues; do not simply add face value without facts or assume every gain is currently recognized. Selling expenses, holding period, liabilities, exclusions and tax-year-specific rules matter. This lesson is conceptual training, not a prepared tax return.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A founder sells shares for $200,000 cash and a $50,000 promissory note. Records show $90,000 original purchase price and $8,000 properly capitalized acquisition costs; there may be selling expenses. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Taxpayer favors installment treatment; examiner challenges the asserted basis and the note's valuation. Determine documentation needed to settle both positions. Identify the exact proposition supported by Internal Revenue Code §§ 61, 1001 and 1012; applicable Treasury regulations, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
        "counterarguments": "Taxpayer favors installment treatment; examiner challenges the asserted basis and the note's valuation. Determine documentation needed to settle both positions.",
        "assignment": "Prepare a calculation worksheet with inputs explicitly marked verified or assumed; write a 700–1,000-word tax research memo addressing § 1001, basis and note uncertainty. Do not invent a final tax bill.",
        "question": "Which information must be established before reliably computing recognized gain?",
        "options": [
          "Amount realized, adjusted basis and applicable recognition rules",
          "Only the buyer's name",
          "Only the original share certificate"
        ],
        "answer": 0,
        "why": "Gain calculation depends on amount realized and adjusted basis; recognition and timing require more analysis.",
        "status": "draft—requires primary-source currency review and qualified assessment"
