        "scenario": "A delivery company pays drivers as contractors while exercising extensive control over routes, schedules and equipment.",
        "reading": "Worker status for federal employment tax can depend on the applicable statutory and common-law framework, including behavioral and financial control and the parties' relationship. Labels in agreements do not determine status. Distinguish employment tax issues from wage law tests and independent state frameworks. Explain withholding, reporting, relief provisions and potential exposure without fabricating a tax amount. Worked method: identify the procedural posture, list the rule elements, write a fact-supported argument for each side, evaluate missing evidence, and explain the possible outcomes without claiming that jurisdictional questions are settled. Confirm the controlling versions of primary authorities before using this work in practice.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A delivery company pays drivers as contractors while exercising extensive control over routes, schedules and equipment. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Company emphasizes independent scheduling; drivers point to control, supervision and inability to negotiate rates. Identify the exact proposition supported by Internal Revenue Code; applicable Treasury and IRS employment tax rules, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
