        "scenario": "Police stop a driver, handcuff the person, and place them in a patrol car. An officer remarks to another officer that evidence may be destroyed if nobody reveals its location. The driver responds with a location before any Miranda warnings.",
        "reading": "Miranda applies to custodial interrogation. Custody is objective and fact-sensitive; restraints similar to formal arrest matter, but not every roadside interaction necessarily constitutes custody. Under Innis, interrogation includes express questioning and its functional equivalent—words or actions police should know are reasonably likely to elicit an incriminating response, excluding words normally attendant to arrest and custody. Evaluate officer intent only insofar as relevant to reasonable foreseeability. Distinguish voluntariness, public-safety exceptions, physical evidence issues and whether a statement is used in the prosecution's case-in-chief. Verify later controlling law.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Police stop a driver, handcuff the person, and place them in a patrol car. An officer remarks to another officer that evidence may be destroyed if nobody reveals its location. The driver responds with a location before any Miranda warnings. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Defense stresses custody and the foreseeable effect of the comment; prosecution argues a spontaneous volunteered statement and a bona fide officer discussion. Changed facts: officers knew where a loaded weapon was accessible to children. Identify the exact proposition supported by Miranda v. Arizona, 384 U.S. 436 (1966); Rhode Island v. Innis, 446 U.S. 291 (1980), check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
